module wiwd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

// The Western District of Wisconsin has no local criminal rules: its
// Local Rules are five rules (W.D. Wis. L.R. 1 to 5, dated 5/2018), and
// none of them states a period for a criminal case, so this layer
// declares no deadline and lit/frcrimp's FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act and
// FRAP 4(b) periods govern unchanged. What the five rules say of a
// criminal case is recorded here only. The magistrate judges are
// designated to hear and determine any pretrial matter except, among
// others, a defendant's motion to dismiss or quash an indictment or
// information and a motion to suppress evidence in a criminal case, and
// on those motions and on applications for post-trial relief by persons
// convicted of criminal offenses they conduct hearings and submit
// proposed findings and recommendations to a judge (W.D. Wis. L.R. 2(A),
// (B)); no one contacts a juror, before or after impanelment, without the
// trial judge's or magistrate judge's prior permission (W.D. Wis. L.R.
// 4); papers are filed, signed and served electronically as the court's
// Administrative Procedures for Electronic Filing and Service require,
// which are not vendored (W.D. Wis. L.R. 5). Lawyer admission and
// discipline (W.D. Wis. L.R. 1) are not part of the criminal case, and
// alternative dispute resolution (W.D. Wis. L.R. 3) is for civil cases.
// The time for objecting to a magistrate judge's recommendation is
// FRCrimP 59(b)(2)'s, in lit/frcrimp, not a local period.
//
// The earlier W.D. Wis. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// are FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act or constitutional authority and belong to
// lit/frcrimp; they are removed here: no reporter text is vendored.
